WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.400 --> 00:00:08.914
Imagine a Torrance corridor project that adds homes, storefronts, a new driveway, and a bus stop used by workers and students.

2
00:00:08.914 --> 00:00:11.956
The traffic memo says vehicles can enter and leave.

3
00:00:11.956 --> 00:00:15.713
A transit rider asks whether the bus will still reach the curb.

4
00:00:15.713 --> 00:00:19.351
A parent asks whether a child can cross the widened driveway.

5
00:00:19.351 --> 00:00:22.393
A firefighter asks whether the queue blocks access.

6
00:00:22.393 --> 00:00:29.311
Project review is strongest when it tests how the place works for all users, not only whether cars can pass through.

7
00:00:29.311 --> 00:00:35.108
This lesson is preparation for public service, not legal or engineering advice about a project.

8
00:00:35.108 --> 00:00:41.209
Safety standards, transit plans, accessibility requirements, CEQA rules, and local authority change.

9
00:00:41.209 --> 00:00:49.385
Use current adopted plans, agency standards, field evidence, qualified technical analysis, and advice from the Torrance city attorney.

10
00:00:49.385 --> 00:00:51.338
Begin with the safe-system idea.

11
00:00:51.338 --> 00:00:55.792
People make mistakes, and bodies have limited tolerance for crash energy.

12
00:00:55.792 --> 00:01:02.138
A transportation system should be designed so ordinary mistakes do not become fatal or serious injuries.

13
00:01:02.138 --> 00:01:11.596
That approach shifts attention from blaming an individual after a collision to managing speed, conflict, exposure, vehicle design, and post-crash response.

14
00:01:11.596 --> 00:01:15.623
For land-use review, translate that principle into a conflict map.

15
00:01:15.623 --> 00:01:24.470
Mark every place where paths cross: driveways, alleys, loading zones, bus stops, bicycle routes, sidewalks, parking entrances, and intersections.

16
00:01:24.470 --> 00:01:28.497
Identify the users, direction, speed, visibility, and time of day.

17
00:01:28.497 --> 00:01:33.745
A driveway that looks quiet at noon may conflict with a bus queue at the evening peak.

18
00:01:33.745 --> 00:01:38.870
Speed is especially important because crash energy rises rapidly as speed increases.

19
00:01:38.870 --> 00:01:42.226
Posted speed alone does not describe actual conditions.

20
00:01:42.226 --> 00:01:48.755
Street width, turning radius, signal timing, lighting, land use, and driver expectation influence behavior.

21
00:01:48.755 --> 00:01:53.331
Ask for observed or modeled operating evidence appropriate to the decision.

22
00:01:53.331 --> 00:01:56.103
Use a project safety screen with five layers.

23
00:01:56.103 --> 00:01:57.889
The first is network context.

24
00:01:57.889 --> 00:02:04.234
Is the site on a high-injury network, a designated truck route, a school route, or an evacuation route?

25
00:02:04.234 --> 00:02:05.651
The second is exposure.

26
00:02:05.651 --> 00:02:10.826
How many people walk, bike, wait for transit, cross, drive, or use mobility devices?

27
00:02:10.826 --> 00:02:12.735
The third is conflict geometry.

28
00:02:12.735 --> 00:02:16.062
Where do movements cross, and at what angle and speed?

29
00:02:16.062 --> 00:02:17.663
The fourth is consequence.

30
00:02:17.663 --> 00:02:20.558
Who is most vulnerable if something goes wrong?

31
00:02:20.558 --> 00:02:21.975
The fifth is treatment.

32
00:02:21.975 --> 00:02:26.534
Can the project eliminate, separate, slow, or clearly manage the conflict?

33
00:02:26.534 --> 00:02:28.813
Do not rely only on reported crashes.

34
00:02:28.813 --> 00:02:33.679
A location with few recorded crashes may still be dangerous if people avoid it.

35
00:02:33.679 --> 00:02:40.455
Near misses, excessive speed, long crossing distance, missing curb ramps, and rider testimony can reveal risk.

36
00:02:40.455 --> 00:02:45.014
At the same time, anecdote alone should not determine a technical finding.

37
00:02:45.014 --> 00:02:49.449
Combine community knowledge with transparent data and field observation.

38
00:02:49.449 --> 00:02:57.765
Quick-build treatments can test improvements with paint, posts, signs, signal changes, temporary curb extensions, or modular materials.

39
00:02:57.765 --> 00:03:06.143
SCAG's public training has shown how quick-build work can advance complete streets and how art can support active-transportation safety.

40
00:03:06.143 --> 00:03:14.644
A temporary project still needs accessibility, maintenance, emergency-response coordination, evaluation, and a clear path to modification.

41
00:03:14.644 --> 00:03:16.184
Now add reliable transit.

42
00:03:16.184 --> 00:03:21.605
Reliability means riders can expect service to arrive and move with tolerable variation.

43
00:03:21.605 --> 00:03:27.149
Frequency matters, but a frequent bus trapped in variable delay is difficult to depend on.

44
00:03:27.149 --> 00:03:37.374
Bus lanes, queue jumps, transit-signal priority, stop consolidation, curb management, all-door boarding, and enforcement can improve performance in the right setting.

45
00:03:37.374 --> 00:03:47.415
SCAG's January 2026 Toolbox Tuesday, Clearing the Lane: New Tools for Reliable Bus Service, presented practical strategies for improving bus speed and reliability.

46
00:03:47.415 --> 00:03:51.727
The planning lesson is that curb and lane choices are service choices.

47
00:03:51.727 --> 00:03:59.797
A loading zone, parking space, turn lane, or driveway may consume the same scarce street space a bus needs to operate consistently.

48
00:03:59.797 --> 00:04:05.772
A project condition should connect to an impact or applicable standard and remain within legal limits.

49
00:04:05.772 --> 00:04:09.813
Avoid using one development to solve every historic corridor problem.

50
00:04:09.813 --> 00:04:16.491
Identify the project's contribution, available authority, nexus, proportionality, funding, and responsible agency.

51
00:04:16.491 --> 00:04:24.984
Some improvements belong in a city capital program, a transit-agency project, a fee program, or a corridor plan rather than a single entitlement.

52
00:04:24.984 --> 00:04:27.503
Coordinate early with the transit operator.

53
00:04:27.503 --> 00:04:37.285
Ask whether the stop will move, whether the bus can align with the curb, whether shelters preserve accessible clearance, and whether turning buses can reenter traffic.

54
00:04:37.285 --> 00:04:41.503
Check boarding areas, lighting, shade, wayfinding, and pedestrian paths.

55
00:04:41.503 --> 00:04:47.126
A beautiful shelter that a wheelchair user cannot reach is not a successful transit improvement.

56
00:04:47.126 --> 00:04:51.402
Reliable service also depends on operations outside the project boundary.

57
00:04:51.402 --> 00:04:54.565
Signal priority needs compatible equipment and policy.

58
00:04:54.565 --> 00:04:57.611
A bus lane needs enforcement and network continuity.

59
00:04:57.611 --> 00:04:59.954
A stop change needs rider communication.

60
00:04:59.954 --> 00:05:05.987
A commission should distinguish what the applicant can deliver from what partner agencies must operate.

61
00:05:05.987 --> 00:05:11.962
For practical application, create a movement-and-reliability audit for the fictional corridor project.

62
00:05:11.962 --> 00:05:15.594
Draw a simple site diagram using only public base information.

63
00:05:15.594 --> 00:05:17.175
Number each conflict point.

64
00:05:17.175 --> 00:05:23.501
For every point, list users, expected volume, operating speed, sight line, control, and potential treatment.

65
00:05:23.501 --> 00:05:28.539
Then trace a rider's path from the nearest origin across the sidewalk to the bus door.

66
00:05:28.539 --> 00:05:35.509
Time that path conceptually in four conditions: normal traffic, peak congestion, nighttime, and a mobility-device trip.

67
00:05:35.509 --> 00:05:37.091
Record missing information.

68
00:05:37.091 --> 00:05:43.300
Add the bus operator's needs, including stop length, curb access, dwell, reentry, and layover if relevant.

69
00:05:43.300 --> 00:05:50.739
Finish with a responsibility matrix naming the applicant, city, transit operator, utility, or regional partner for each action.

70
00:05:50.739 --> 00:05:52.630
Use predictive tools carefully.

71
00:05:52.630 --> 00:06:04.038
SCAG's 2025 and 2026 public Toolbox Tuesday sessions introduced transportation-safety resources and a predictive modeling platform intended to help local jurisdictions target investments.

72
00:06:04.038 --> 00:06:10.870
Predictive results can reveal road features associated with risk, including places without a long crash history.

73
00:06:10.870 --> 00:06:15.445
They do not replace field review, community input, or engineering judgment.

74
00:06:15.445 --> 00:06:21.241
Document the model version, data period, limitations, and how the output affected the decision.

75
00:06:21.241 --> 00:06:22.949
Equity belongs in the audit.

76
00:06:22.949 --> 00:06:27.097
Transit delay costs time and can threaten a worker's job connection.

77
00:06:27.097 --> 00:06:30.696
Missing shade can make a stop unusable during extreme heat.

78
00:06:30.696 --> 00:06:34.844
A long detour can burden an older adult or person with a disability.

79
00:06:34.844 --> 00:06:38.199
Enforcement strategies can impose unequal consequences.

80
00:06:38.199 --> 00:06:44.788
Ask who receives the benefit, who bears construction or enforcement burdens, and who participated in design.

81
00:06:44.788 --> 00:06:47.106
Measure outcomes after implementation.

82
00:06:47.106 --> 00:06:52.840
Safety metrics can include speed, yielding, conflicts, crossings, and severe-collision trends.

83
00:06:52.840 --> 00:06:58.880
Transit metrics can include travel time, variability, on-time performance, dwell, and rider access.

84
00:06:58.880 --> 00:07:01.869
Set a review date and a threshold for adjustment.

85
00:07:01.869 --> 00:07:06.810
A project is not evidence-based merely because data was used before construction.

86
00:07:06.810 --> 00:07:08.335
Do not promise zero risk.

87
00:07:08.335 --> 00:07:12.727
State the objective, treatment, expected mechanism, and monitoring plan.

88
00:07:12.727 --> 00:07:20.231
Keep operational claims separate from CEQA significance findings unless the environmental document expressly connects them.

89
00:07:20.231 --> 00:07:21.146
Details change.

90
00:07:21.146 --> 00:07:26.575
Current design standards, operator requirements, adopted plans, and legal advice control.

91
00:07:26.575 --> 00:07:28.203
Now three recall questions.

92
00:07:28.203 --> 00:07:28.987
Question one.

93
00:07:28.987 --> 00:07:31.399
What does a safe-system approach assume?

94
00:07:31.399 --> 00:07:31.821
Answer.

95
00:07:31.821 --> 00:07:40.745
People will make mistakes, so the system should manage speed, conflict, and exposure to prevent those mistakes from causing death or serious injury.

96
00:07:40.745 --> 00:07:41.529
Question two.

97
00:07:41.529 --> 00:07:44.544
Name three tools that can improve bus reliability.

98
00:07:44.544 --> 00:07:44.966
Answer.

99
00:07:44.966 --> 00:07:53.528
Examples include bus lanes, queue jumps, transit-signal priority, curb management, stop changes, all-door boarding, and effective enforcement.

100
00:07:53.528 --> 00:07:54.432
Question three.

101
00:07:54.432 --> 00:07:57.568
Why are reported crashes insufficient by themselves?

102
00:07:57.568 --> 00:07:57.990
Answer.

103
00:07:57.990 --> 00:08:02.331
Avoidance, underreporting, recent change, and near misses can hide risk.

104
00:08:02.331 --> 00:08:09.567
Crash records should be combined with exposure, design, field observation, predictive analysis, and community knowledge.

105
00:08:09.567 --> 00:08:13.185
Your learner artifact is the movement-and-reliability audit.

106
00:08:13.185 --> 00:08:21.627
Include the numbered conflict map, the four-condition rider journey, a responsibility matrix, and three outcome measures with a review date.

107
00:08:21.627 --> 00:08:24.762
Use only public information and label every unknown.

108
00:08:24.762 --> 00:08:42.263
This lesson draws from SCAG's public Toolbox Tuesday materials from 2023 through 2026, including Quick-Build Projects for Roadway Safety and Complete Streets, Arts and Active Transportation Safety, From Risk to Resilience: Utilizing Transportation Safety Resources, the SCAG Transportation Safety Predictive Modeling Platform sessions, Clearing the Lane: New Tools for Reliable Bus Service, and SCAG's transportation project-development training.

109
00:08:42.263 --> 00:08:44.775
It also uses public safe-system and transit-planning principles.

110
00:08:44.775 --> 00:08:48.699
The narration is original and copies no slides, maps, recordings, source prose, or proprietary data.

111
00:08:48.699 --> 00:08:51.956
Confirm current standards, agency requirements, evidence, and city-attorney advice.
